    "updated_at": "2024-12-04T07:57:55Z",
    "body": "@StephenHaney you don't need an rAF loop nor to dirty your layout by resizing this div. `ResizeObserver.observe()`  will queue the RO's observer when it's disconnected, so you just need to disconnect it in the callback and call `observe()` when you need to hook to the next frame.   \r\n\r\nFor reference below is a `waitBrowserReflow` function I use, but note that your points about trashing the layout in this callback and possibly not being the last such callback still stand and thus can't be a proper bullet proof solution for the use case here.\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst activeCallbacks = [];\r\nlet inCallback = false;\r\nconst observer = new ResizeObserver((...entries) => {\r\n  observer.disconnect();\r\n  const callbacks = [...new Set(activeCallbacks)];\r\n  activeCallbacks.length = 0;\r\n  inCallback = true;\r\n  for (const callback of callbacks) {\r\n    try { callback?.(); } finally {}\r\n  }\r\n  inCallback = false;\r\n});\r\n\r\nexport function waitBrowserReflow() {\r\n  if (inCallback) {\r\n    requestAnimationFrame(() => observer.observe(globalThis?.document.body));\r\n  }\r\n  else {\r\n    observer.observe(globalThis?.document.body);\r\n  }\r\n  return new Promise((res) => activeCallbacks.push(res));\r\n}\r\n```",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
